From 2f9bb867e24916de9f45e5bd2e37b6ea7ade46c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 02:52:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 03/14] feat(outbox): retry failed publications with backoff
 and a dead state

A single publish failure marked the event 'failed', and get_pending
only ever returned 'pending' events, so one transient error meant the
event was never delivered. The Dispatcher also kept an in-memory set of
processed ids that grew without bound and was lost on restart.

Failures are now recorded in the database: attempts, last_error and
next_attempt_at, with exponential backoff (RetryPolicy: 1 s doubling up
to 300 s, 10 attempts by default, about 8.5 minutes). After the last
attempt the event moves to 'dead', is logged at ERROR, can be listed
with list_dead() and is put back in the queue with redrive(). The
Dispatcher reads only due events from the database, ends a round at the
first failure so an unreachable target does not cost one timeout per
event, and drops published events after a retention period (7 days).
It gains start()/shutdown() for running as a background task, and
survives database errors in its loop.

Existing databases are migrated in place: the new columns are added and
legacy 'failed' events become 'dead' so they can be redriven.
